B.aWH=new A.L("Move over 3 months under 0.5 pp","Ordinary drift (calm).")
B.hsV=new A.L("Move of 0.5 to 1.0 pp, up or down","Expectations repricing quickly (watch).")
B.hlX=new A.L("Move of 1.0 pp or more, up or down","Disorderly repricing (stress).")
B.hsy=new A.L("Above the policy rate","Markets expect hikes ahead.")
B.hsx=new A.L("Below the policy rate","Markets expect cuts ahead.")
B.cV3=s([B.aWH,B.hsV,B.hlX,B.hsy,B.hsx],t.L)
B.ctq=s(["Inflation or jobs surprise \u2192 the market re-guesses the Fed's next moves.","2-year yield jumps or drops, because it is short enough to track those moves closely.","Mortgage, car-loan and business-loan rates follow, and so do stock valuations."],t.s)
B.hsw=new A.L("2022 (approx. Mar\u2013Oct)","The 2-year rose from near 1% to above 4% as the Fed began its fastest tightening in decades.")
B.hrL=new A.L("Mar 2023","After the Silicon Valley Bank failure the 2-year fell by well over a point in a few days \u2014 a safe-haven rush and a sudden bet on cuts.")
B.cyf=s([B.hsw,B.hrL],t.L)
B.d6g=s(["y10","s2s10","realyield","c-yield-curve"],t.s)
B.fGi=new A.e6("y2","2-year Treasury yield","The interest rate the US government pays to borrow for two years \u2014 the market's running guess at where the Fed's policy rate is heading.","Markets expect the Fed to keep rates higher for longer, or inflation to stay firm. Borrowing gets more expensive.","Markets expect rate cuts, or are running to safety because growth looks weaker.","Rate-hike or higher-for-longer bets are building.","Markets are pricing rate cuts or a growth scare.","At {v}, the 2-year has been moving at an ordinary pace over the last three months.","At {v}, the 2-year has moved about half a point or more in three months \u2014 rate expectations are shifting fast.","At {v}, the 2-year has moved a full point or more in three months \u2014 a violent repricing of the Fed path.",B.cV3,B.ctq,"Rising short yields pressure long-duration growth stocks and housing-linked names; banks with floating-rate loans can benefit.",B.cyf,B.d6g)
B.hmX=new A.L("0.5 to 1.0 pp, up or down","Repricing (watch).")
B.hso=new A.L("1.0 pp or more, up or down","Bond-market shock (stress).")
B.hq_=new A.L("Level vs inflation","Compare with the real yield and breakeven to see which part moved.")
B.d8C=s([B.aWH,B.hmX,B.hso,B.hq_],t.L)
B.cVG=s(["Higher expected inflation, growth or Fed rates \u2192 higher 10-year yield.","The discount rate on future profits rises \u2192 stock valuations compress, most for growth stocks.","Mortgage and corporate borrowing costs rise \u2192 housing and investment slow."],t.s)
B.hny=new A.L("Aug 2020 (approx.)","The 10-year hit an all-time low near 0.5% during the pandemic.")
B.hmB=new A.L("Oct 2023 (approx.)","It reached about 5% for the first time since 2007 as investors demanded more pay for long bonds.")
B.cS4=s([B.hny,B.hmB],t.L)
B.dfs=s(["y2","realyield","termpremium","breakeven","c-yield-curve"],t.s)
B.fGl=new A.e6("y10","10-year Treasury yield","The benchmark long-term interest rate: what the US government pays to borrow for ten years. Mortgage rates and stock valuations key off it.","Growth or inflation expectations are rising, the Fed is expected to stay tight, or investors want more pay for holding long bonds. Existing bond prices fall.","Growth fears, falling inflation expectations or a rush to safety. Existing bond prices rise.","Long-term borrowing costs rising \u2014 pressure on valuations.","Long yields falling \u2014 growth fear or easing inflation.","At {v}, the 10-year has moved at an ordinary pace over the last three months.","At {v}, the 10-year has swung half a point or more in three months \u2014 borrowing costs are repricing.","At {v}, the 10-year has swung a full point or more in three months \u2014 a bond-market shock.",B.d8C,B.cVG,"Rate-sensitive sectors (real estate, utilities, small caps) and long-duration growth stocks feel it most; banks gain from a steeper curve.",B.cS4,B.dfs)
B.aWx=new A.L("Above +0.5 pp","Normal slope (calm).")
B.hoG=new A.L("0 to +0.5 pp","Flat, little cushion (watch).")
B.hrN=new A.L("Below 0 pp","Inverted (stress): a warning that has preceded most US recessions since the 1970s, with a lag of many months.")
B.hsU=new A.L("Re-steepening after an inversion","Often happens as cuts begin; the recession, if any, has tended to start near or after this.")
B.d0i=s([B.aWx,B.hoG,B.hrN,B.hsU],t.L)
B.d6a=s(["Fed holds short rates high to fight inflation.","Investors expect growth to fade and rates to fall later, so long yields stay lower than short yields.","Banks earn less on lending long and borrowing short \u2192 they tighten credit \u2192 growth slows."],t.s)
B.hp4=new A.L("Jul 2022 \u2013 about Sep 2024","Among the longest inversions on record: about two years, with the deepest point near \u22121 pp in mid-2023. No recession had been declared by the end of it \u2014 the signal is a warning, not a clock.")
B.hsD=new A.L("2006\u20132007","The curve inverted in 2006 and the recession began in Dec 2007.")
B.hug=new A.L("Aug 2019","A brief inversion; the 2020 recession was caused by the pandemic, so it is a poor test of the signal.")
B.cW3=s([B.hp4,B.hsD,B.hug],t.L)
B.cST=s(["s3m10","y2","y10","c-yield-curve","c-recession"],t.s)
B.fGO=new A.e6("s2s10","10-year minus 2-year spread","The gap between the 10-year and 2-year Treasury yields \u2014 the most-watched read on the shape of the yield curve.","The curve is steepening: long rates rising faster than short ones. After an inversion this usually comes as the Fed cuts, and it often arrives close to the start of a slowdown.","The curve is flattening, and below zero it is inverted: the market sees tighter policy now and weaker growth later.","Curve steepening \u2014 often follows an inversion as cuts arrive.","Curve flattening toward or deeper into inversion.","At {v}, the curve has a normal upward slope \u2014 the market is not signalling near-term trouble.","At {v}, the curve is nearly flat \u2014 it is close to the point where short rates exceed long ones.","At {v}, the curve is inverted (short rates above long rates), historically an early recession warning.",B.d0i,B.d6a,"Banks are the textbook loser from inversion; defensives such as utilities and staples tend to lead when it persists.",B.cW3,B.cST)
B.hla=new A.L("0 to +0.5 pp","Flat (watch).")
B.hjB=new A.L("Below 0 pp","Inverted (stress). Academic and Fed research has found this spread, with a lead of roughly a year, to be a strong recession indicator in US data.")
B.cXc=s([B.aWx,B.hla,B.hjB],t.L)
B.cX5=s(["The 3-month bill tracks the Fed's policy rate almost directly.","When it is above the 10-year yield, policy is tighter than the market's long-run view.","That squeezes bank margins and credit growth, which is how it may feed through to the economy."],t.s)
B.ho7=new A.L("Oct 2022 \u2013 about late 2024","Inverted for roughly two years, again without a declared recession by the end.")
B.huu=new A.L("2006\u20132007","Inverted for much of 2006 and 2007; recession began Dec 2007.")
B.cQY=s([B.ho7,B.huu],t.L)
B.cNU=s(["s2s10","sahm","c-yield-curve","c-recession"],t.s)
B.fGG=new A.e6("s3m10","10-year minus 3-month spread","The gap between the 10-year yield and the 3-month Treasury bill yield \u2014 the curve measure the New York Fed uses in its recession-probability model.","The curve is steepening. Short rates are falling toward cuts or long rates are rising.","The curve is flattening or inverting: policy is tight relative to the long-run outlook.","Curve steepening \u2014 short rates easing or long rates rising.","Curve flattening toward inversion.","At {v}, the curve has a normal slope.","At {v}, the curve is close to flat.","At {v}, the curve is inverted \u2014 the version of the signal with the best long-run record.",B.cXc,B.cX5,"Same as the 10y\u20132y curve: banks and cyclicals feel it first.",B.cQY,B.cNU)
B.hns=new A.L("Below 1.5%","Moderate (calm).")
B.hp_=new A.L("1.5% to 2.5%","Restrictive (watch).")
B.hoc=new A.L("2.5% or more","Very restrictive for the post-2008 era (stress).")
B.hjL=new A.L("Negative","Savers lose to inflation; fuels hunts for yield (2020\u201321).")
B.d0z=s([B.hns,B.hp_,B.hoc,B.hjL],t.L)
B.d3M=s(["Real yield = nominal 10-year yield \u2212 the market's expected inflation (the breakeven).","A higher real yield raises the bar every investment must clear.","Assets that pay nothing today (gold) or most profit far in the future (growth stocks) lose appeal."],t.s)
B.hlS=new A.L("Dec 2021 (approx.)","The real yield sat near \u22121% as the Fed was still at zero rates.")
B.huW=new A.L("Oct 2023 (approx.)","It reached about 2.5%, the highest since 2007, as equities sold off.")
B.d35=s([B.hlS,B.huW],t.L)
B.dbr=s(["y10","breakeven","gold","c-real-nominal"],t.s)
B.fHt=new A.e6("realyield","10-year real yield (TIPS)","The 10-year Treasury yield after inflation, read from inflation-protected bonds (TIPS). It is the true cost of money once rising prices are stripped out.","Money is getting more expensive in real terms: bad for gold and long-duration growth stocks, good for savers.","Real borrowing costs are easing: supportive for gold and growth stocks.","Real borrowing costs rising \u2014 a headwind for gold and growth.","Real yields easing \u2014 a tailwind for gold and growth.","At {v}, the real yield is moderate.","At {v}, the real yield is high enough to weigh on valuations and gold.","At {v}, the real yield is at the high end of the post-2008 range \u2014 a restrictive setting.",B.d0z,B.d3M,"Long-duration tech and unprofitable growth are most sensitive; gold miners follow gold.",B.d35,B.dbr)
B.hpm=new A.L("Within the middle 80% of history","Ordinary (calm).")
B.hnZ=new A.L("Estimate caveat","Different models give different levels; read direction, not decimals.")
B.d0y=s([B.hpm,B.ka,B.k9,B.hnZ],t.L)
B.dcJ=s(["10-year yield = expected average short rate + term premium.","When the premium rises, the yield rises even if rate expectations do not change.","Higher long yields hit mortgages and valuations without any Fed move."],t.s)
B.hru=new A.L("2019\u20132022 (approx.)","The estimate was negative for much of the period \u2014 heavy central-bank bond buying pushed the premium below zero.")
B.hsQ=new A.L("2023 (approx.)","It turned positive and climbed as the Fed shrank its balance sheet and Treasury issuance rose.")
B.d0A=s([B.hru,B.hsQ],t.L)
B.djW=s(["y10","realyield","fedbs"],t.s)
B.fHe=new A.e6("termpremium","10-year term premium","The extra yield investors demand for locking money into a 10-year bond instead of rolling short ones. It is an estimate (here from the New York Fed's ACM model), not something quoted in the market.","Investors want more pay for the risk of holding long bonds \u2014 because of heavy government borrowing, inflation uncertainty or weaker demand.","Long bonds are seen as safe or in high demand (central-bank buying, flight to safety).","Investors demanding more pay to hold long bonds.","Long bonds in demand \u2014 premium shrinking.","At {v}, the term premium is in an ordinary range for its history.","At {v}, the term premium is unusually high or low against its own history.","At {v}, the term premium is at an extreme of its own history.",B.d0y,B.dcJ,"Same channels as the 10-year yield: housing, utilities, long-duration growth.",B.d0A,B.djW)
B.ho2=new A.L("Below 3%","Near target (calm). The Fed's 2% goal is defined on PCE, which usually runs a little below CPI.")
B.hlE=new A.L("3% to 5%","Above target (watch).")
B.hl_=new A.L("5% or more","High (stress).")
B.hkv=new A.L("Below 0%","Deflation: prices falling, a different problem.")
