B.d7_=s(["Companies see weaker demand and slow hiring, then cut staff.","Fewer paychecks \u2192 less spending \u2192 weaker sales \u2192 more layoffs.","The Fed may cut rates to support jobs, easing borrowing costs."],t.s)
B.hnV=new A.L("Apr 2020","Unemployment jumped to about 14.8% in a single month at the start of the pandemic, the highest in the data, which begin in 1948.")
B.hsz=new A.L("Oct 2009 (approx.)","It peaked near 10% after the financial crisis.")
B.hsH=new A.L("Early 2023","It reached about 3.4%, the lowest since 1969.")
B.ckX=s([B.hnV,B.hsz,B.hsH],t.L)
B.cX2=s(["sahm","claims","s2s10","c-recession"],t.s)
B.fHp=new A.e6("unrate","Unemployment rate","The share of people in the labor force who are looking for work and cannot find it. Published monthly by the Bureau of Labor Statistics.","Hiring is slowing or layoffs are rising. A fast rise is one of the clearest signs a slowdown has started.","The labor market is tightening: jobs are easy to find, wages tend to rise.","Joblessness rising \u2014 labor market loosening.","Joblessness falling \u2014 labor market tightening.","At {v}, unemployment has not risen meaningfully over the past year.","At {v}, unemployment is up half a point or more from a year ago \u2014 the labor market is softening.","At {v}, unemployment is up a full point or more from a year ago \u2014 a pattern seen in recessions.",B.cMr,B.d7_,"Consumer discretionary, housing and retail feel the spending drop first.",B.ckX,B.cX2)
B.hnz=new A.L("Below 0.3 pp","No signal (calm).")
B.hot=new A.L("0.3 to 0.5 pp","Approaching the trigger (watch).")
B.hs4=new A.L("0.5 pp or more","Rule triggered (stress). Since 1970 it has fired at or near the start of every US recession.")
B.hqf=new A.L("Caveat","It is a rule fitted to history, not a law. In Aug 2024 it fired while the labor force was growing fast, and its author argued that this time was different.")
B.d1i=s([B.hnz,B.hot,B.hs4,B.hqf],t.L)
B.cgp=s(["Unemployment rising this fast has historically fed on itself: layoffs \u2192 lower spending \u2192 more layoffs.","The rule is simple on purpose, so it is hard to overfit."],t.s)
B.hnn=new A.L("Aug 2024","The real-time indicator crossed 0.5 for the first time since 2020. The NBER had not dated a recession by the end of that year.")
B.hnp=new A.L("Dec 2007 and Mar 2020","The rule fired within months of the recession start dates.")
B.dcG=s([B.hnn,B.hnp],t.L)
B.c9A=s(["unrate","claims","s3m10","c-recession"],t.s)
B.fH5=new A.e6("sahm","Sahm rule recession indicator","A rule that flags a recession when the 3-month average unemployment rate rises 0.5 percentage points or more above its lowest level of the prior 12 months. Created by economist Claudia Sahm; the series here is the real-time version.","Unemployment is climbing quickly relative to its recent low. At 0.5 the rule is triggered.","The labor market is stabilizing or improving.","Sahm gap widening \u2014 unemployment rising fast.","Sahm gap narrowing \u2014 labor market steadier.","At {v}, unemployment is not rising meaningfully above its recent low.","At {v}, unemployment is rising above its recent low \u2014 getting close to the 0.5 trigger.","At {v}, the Sahm rule is at or past its 0.5 trigger.",B.d1i,B.cgp,"Cyclical sectors and small caps tend to lag when labor weakens.",B.dcG,B.c9A)
B.hsP=new A.L("Below 250k a week","Low layoffs (calm).")
B.hos=new A.L("250k to 350k","Elevated (watch).")
B.hmp=new A.L("350k or more","Recession-type (stress).")
B.hun=new A.L("Seasonal quirks","Holidays and auto-plant shutdowns can add short noise; look at the 4-week trend.")
B.d0r=s([B.hsP,B.hos,B.hmp,B.hun],t.L)
B.cPD=s(["Firms cut staff \u2192 filings jump.","Weeks later the unemployment rate rises and spending drops.","Because it is weekly, claims turn before monthly jobs data."],t.s)
B.hs3=new A.L("Late Mar 2020","Weekly claims hit about 6 million, by far a record, in the first weeks of pandemic shutdowns.")
B.hku=new A.L("Mar 2009 (approx.)","Weekly claims peaked near 660,000 in the financial crisis.")
B.df8=s([B.hs3,B.hku],t.L)
B.fGo=new A.e6("claims","Initial jobless claims","The number of people filing for unemployment insurance for the first time that week. Published every Thursday, so it is the fastest read on layoffs.","More layoffs. A sustained climb often comes before the unemployment rate rises.","Few layoffs: employers are holding on to workers.","Layoff filings climbing.","Layoff filings easing.","At {v} a week, layoffs are low.","At {v} a week, layoff filings are elevated.","At {v} a week, layoff filings are at recession-like levels.",B.d0r,B.cPD,"Retail, transport and manufacturing layoffs show here first.",B.df8,B.aBn)
B.ho1=new A.L("\u22122% to +1%","Flat to soft (watch).")
B.hjM=new A.L("Scope","Only about a fifth of the economy is industrial; services drive most jobs.")
B.cJF=s([B.aWZ,B.ho1,B.aWS,B.hjM],t.L)
B.dc4=s(["Orders fall \u2192 factories cut output and hours.","Suppliers and truckers see less volume \u2192 cyclical earnings drop."],t.s)
B.hjz=new A.L("2008\u20132009","Output fell sharply year over year through the financial crisis.")
B.hqO=new A.L("Mar\u2013Apr 2020","Output dropped by double digits in two months, then recovered over the following year.")
B.cSb=s([B.hjz,B.hqO],t.L)
B.fGC=new A.e6("indpro","Industrial production (year over year)","The yearly change in the output of US factories, mines and utilities, from the Federal Reserve's industrial production index.","Factories are producing more: demand for goods is solid.","Factory output is shrinking \u2014 often a sign of weak demand or a goods-sector recession.","Factory output growing.","Factory output shrinking.","At {v} over the year, industrial output is growing.","At {v} over the year, industrial output is flat or only barely growing.","At {v} over the year, industrial output is contracting \u2014 a pattern seen around recessions.",B.cJF,B.dc4,"Materials, industrials, transport and commodity producers.",B.cSb,B.axW)
B.hll=new A.L("Below 5%","Calm (calm). Spreads below about 4% mean credit is cheap and easy.")
B.hmr=new A.L("5% to 7%","Wider than normal (watch).")
B.ho4=new A.L("7% or more","Stress (stress): lenders are pulling back.")
B.hnj=new A.L("Direction","A fast widening matters more than the level.")
B.cyb=s([B.hll,B.hmr,B.ho4,B.hnj],t.L)
B.dhN=s(["Fear of defaults \u2192 investors sell junk bonds \u2192 spreads widen.","Companies find borrowing costlier \u2192 they cut investment and hiring.","Widening spreads often lead equity weakness by days or weeks."],t.s)
B.htG=new A.L("Dec 2008 (approx.)","The spread peaked near 20% in the financial crisis.")
B.hqN=new A.L("Mar 2020","It jumped to about 11% within weeks, then fell as the Fed backed credit markets.")
B.hm6=new A.L("2007 and 2024\u201325 (approx.)","Spreads sat near 2.5% \u2014 the tight end of their history.")
B.det=s([B.htG,B.hqN,B.hm6],t.L)
B.cvr=s(["igoas","vix","nfci","c-credit-spread"],t.s)
B.fHa=new A.e6("hyoas","High-yield credit spread","The extra yield that risky (junk-rated) US companies pay over Treasuries (option-adjusted spread, ICE BofA index). It is the price of default fear.","Investors are demanding more pay to lend to risky companies \u2014 worry about defaults is rising, usually alongside falling stocks.","Credit is easy and fear is low. Very low spreads can also mean complacency.","Junk-bond fear rising \u2014 credit tightening.","Credit spreads tightening \u2014 risk appetite healthy.","At {v}, the junk-bond market is calm.","At {v}, credit spreads are wider than usual.","At {v}, credit spreads are at stress levels.",B.cyb,B.dhN,"Energy, leveraged companies and small caps are most exposed.",B.det,B.cvr)
B.hnh=new A.L("Below 1.8%","Calm (calm).")
B.htx=new A.L("1.8% to 2.8%","Wider than usual (watch).")
B.hlh=new A.L("2.8% or more","Stress (stress).")
B.cQA=s([B.hnh,B.htx,B.hlh],t.L)
B.d1c=s(["Investment-grade debt is the backbone of corporate funding.","When its spread widens, the whole economy's borrowing cost rises."],t.s)
B.hjO=new A.L("Late 2008 (approx.)","Investment-grade spreads peaked near 6%.")
B.hmJ=new A.L("Mar 2020 (approx.)","They jumped to about 4% before the Fed's corporate-bond support.")
B.cKE=s([B.hjO,B.hmJ],t.L)
B.cMf=s(["hyoas","c-credit-spread","nfci"],t.s)
B.fGz=new A.e6("igoas","Investment-grade credit spread","The extra yield that high-quality (investment-grade) US companies pay over Treasuries (option-adjusted spread, ICE BofA index).","Even strong companies are being asked to pay more to borrow: stress is spreading beyond junk.","Healthy corporate credit.","Even high-grade credit widening.","High-grade credit tightening.","At {v}, investment-grade credit is calm.","At {v}, even high-grade borrowers are paying more.","At {v}, high-grade credit is under real strain.",B.cQA,B.d1c,"Financials and utilities (large issuers) are most affected.",B.cKE,B.cMf)
B.hqx=new A.L("Below 20","Calm (calm). The long-run average is close to 19\u201320.")
B.hml=new A.L("20 to 30","Elevated (watch).")
B.hn3=new A.L("30 or more","Stress (stress).")
B.huI=new A.L("Spikes fade","VIX tends to mean-revert quickly; the term structure shows whether fear is lasting.")
B.cY5=s([B.hqx,B.hml,B.hn3,B.huI],t.L)
B.cXi=s(["Stocks fall \u2192 investors buy puts \u2192 option prices rise \u2192 VIX jumps.","Funds that target volatility sell stocks as it rises, which can add to the move."],t.s)
B.hrm=new A.L("Oct 2008","VIX closed near 81 in the financial crisis.")
B.hkb=new A.L("Mar 2020","It closed near 83, a record close, in the pandemic crash.")
B.hqe=new A.L("Aug 5 2024","VIX spiked intraday above 60 and closed near 39 as the yen carry trade unwound, then faded within days.")
B.c8W=s([B.hrm,B.hkb,B.hqe],t.L)
B.fGL=new A.e6("vix","VIX volatility index",'The market\'s expected 30-day swing in the S&P 500, backed out of option prices. Often called the "fear gauge".',"Investors are paying up for protection: stocks usually fall when this spikes.","Calm markets. Very low readings can reflect complacency.","Fear gauge rising \u2014 hedging demand up.","Fear gauge falling \u2014 markets calming.","At {v}, expected market volatility is below its usual stress zone.","At {v}, expected volatility is elevated \u2014 hedging demand is up.","At {v}, the fear gauge is in the stress zone.",B.cY5,B.cXi,"Equity-wide; high-beta growth and small caps swing most.",B.c8W,B.aBD)
B.hq1=new A.L("Below 0.95","Normal (calm).")
B.hsk=new A.L("0.95 to 1.0","Flattening (watch).")
B.hlZ=new A.L("1.0 or more","Backwardation (stress): usually seen in panics, and it tends to be short-lived.")
B.cNY=s([B.hq1,B.hsk,B.hlZ],t.L)
B.cU1=s(["A shock \u2192 near-term options reprice faster than longer-dated ones.","When VIX rises above VIX3M, traders are paying for immediate protection."],t.s)
B.hum=new A.L("Mar 2020","The term structure inverted sharply in the pandemic crash.")
B.huT=new A.L("Aug 2024 (approx.)","A brief inversion during the August volatility spike.")
B.cVm=s([B.hum,B.huT],t.L)
B.fGZ=new A.e6("vixterm","VIX term structure (VIX \xf7 3-month VIX)","The 30-day VIX divided by the 3-month VIX (VIX3M). Normally below 1: near-term fear is lower than medium-term uncertainty.","Near-term fear is rising toward or above medium-term fear.","Normal calm: short-term fear is below medium-term fear.","Short-term fear rising vs longer-term.","Term structure normalizing.","At {v}, the term structure has its normal shape \u2014 near-term fear is below longer-term fear.","At {v}, near-term fear is almost as high as longer-term fear.","At {v}, the term structure is inverted (backwardation): the market fears the next month more than the next quarter.",B.cNY,B.cU1,"Equity-wide.",B.cVm,B.aBC)
B.hpJ=new A.L("Below 0","Looser than average (calm).")
B.hs7=new A.L("0 to +0.5","Tightening (watch).")
B.hnI=new A.L("+0.5 or more","Tight (stress).")
B.huC=new A.L("Why it helps","It combines many signals, so one noisy market does not mislead.")
B.djM=s([B.hpJ,B.hs7,B.hnI,B.huC],t.L)
B.d7g=s(["Tight conditions raise borrowing costs for everyone.","Firms and households borrow and spend less \u2192 growth slows."],t.s)
B.hqu=new A.L("2008","The index reached its tightest levels of the series in the financial crisis.")
B.htv=new A.L("Mar 2020","Conditions tightened sharply, then eased within weeks as the Fed stepped in.")
B.d1k=s([B.hqu,B.htv],t.L)
B.fGX=new A.e6("nfci","Financial conditions (NFCI)","The Chicago Fed's National Financial Conditions Index: a weekly blend of 105 measures of risk, credit and leverage. Zero means average conditions; positive means tighter than average.","Financial conditions are tightening: credit costs more and is harder to get.","Conditions are loosening: money is cheap and easy to get.","Financial conditions tightening.","Financial conditions loosening.","At {v}, conditions are looser than average \u2014 financing is easy.","At {v}, conditions are at or just past average tightness.","At {v}, financial conditions are clearly tight.",B.djM,B.d7g,"Banks, real estate, leveraged firms.",B.d1k,B.azo)
B.dkD=s([B.fHp,B.fH5,B.fGo,B.fGC,B.fHa,B.fGz,B.fGL,B.fGZ,B.fGX],t.Vh)
